Keying simulations
==================

Runs of the iambic keyer at 30 WPM, one plot each. Three sets are kept
here, and paddle_sweep.py can produce a fourth.

Each plot has three panels against one time axis, zeroed on the paddle
that closes first:

  - what the operator did: the two paddles' closures
  - what the operator heard: the sidetone as rendered, each tone named
    and carrying the length it actually came out at
  - what the K4 was sent: every KZD and KZU, key down above the line
    and key up below

Every tone comes out at exactly the length its KZU carries -- 40ms or
120ms, across all four scenarios and every run of them. The sidetone
catch-up shortens tones a fraction where the audio has drifted past the
lag the keyer holds, and over the device these were run against it
never has: the delay sits at what was measured of it. A device that
drifts would show the tones a shade under, and the K4 still told the
full length, so nothing on the air is ever short.


keying_30wpm_swap_*.png -- twenty-one runs
------------------------------------------

The dit paddle closes at time zero, and at the moment named in the
file's name the operator releases the dit paddle and closes the dah,
holding it. That moment moves 4ms per run, from 84ms to 164ms.

What they are here to show is that once the first dah has gone out, no
dit follows it. Across the sweep the only thing that varies is how many
dits precede the switch, which is what should vary:

     84-104ms   dit dah dah dah
    108-164ms   dit dit dah dah dah

Before the dit up memory was made to require that the paddle had also
been closed during the element it is remembered from, 13 of these 21
runs put an unasked-for dit after the first dah -- as element 3 below
108ms and as element 4 from 140ms up.

Summary: summary_swap.json


keying_30wpm_dit-tap_*.png -- fifty-six runs
--------------------------------------------

The mirror of the dah tap below: the dah paddle closes at time zero and
is held for the whole run, and the dit paddle is tapped for 5ms at the
moment named in the file's name. That moment moves 4ms per run, from
80ms to 300ms -- nearly five dah element periods, a dah being three
times a dit.

Every run inserts exactly one dit into the stream of dahs, never two
and never none, and the dahs resume after it. What varies is only which
element the dit becomes:

     80-184ms   dah dit dah dah dah dah
    188-300ms   dah dah dit dah dah dah

The boundary sits at 188ms, not at the 160ms where the second dah
begins. The 28ms between the two is send_burst asking the paddles a
second time: an element is started before it is known what it is, and
read again once its sidetone can be heard, so a tap arriving up to
184ms still catches the element that began at 160ms. That second
reading is what the band either side of a boundary is made of.

Summary: summary_dit-tap.json


keying_30wpm_squeeze_*.png -- a hundred and twenty-five runs
------------------------------------------------------------

Both paddles closed at time zero and both let go together at the moment
named in the file's name, moving 4ms per run from 4ms to 500ms. The
squeeze alternates on a 240ms cycle at this speed, so that is two whole
cycles and a little over: the release falls in every part of one, and
the pattern is then seen to repeat rather than assumed to.

    release       released during   elements sent
      4-104ms     the first dit     dit dah
    108-264ms     the first dah     dit dah dit
    268-344ms     the second dit    dit dah dit dah
    348-500ms     the second dah    dit dah dit dah dit

Each band ends exactly where the next tone becomes audible: the second
at 104ms, the third at 264, the fourth at 344. The 4ms step lands on
all three, so in three runs of the sweep the squeeze is let go of at
the very instant a tone begins, and all three fall the same way -- into
the band ending rather than the one starting. That is the rule below
working as written, and it is why the sweep is cut from the sidetone
and not from the keyed grid.

This is the case iambic B exists for. The rule, stated against what the
operator can hear: a squeeze let go of while an element's sidetone is
sounding, or during the space after it, owes exactly one more element --
the opposite of that one -- and nothing after it.

Alone among the sets here this one is checked as well as drawn. The
heard timeline is cut at each tone's start, so a window is a tone plus
the space following it and every release falls in exactly one. Each run
says which element it was released during, what was owed for it, and
what actually followed, and the run ends with a count. All of them follow
the rule.

Getting all of them took three things, and the sweep is what said each
of them was needed. It does not press equally on all three now: the
second has since been made good another way, and what is left of the
case for it is in the paragraph itself.

The memories measure a paddle against the previous element, and they
measure it from when that element was heard, not from when it was
keyed. The hand works to the sidetone: an element is not there to be
answered, nor to be still squeezing through, until it can be heard.
Measured from the keyed start instead, 24 of the 125 go wrong -- the
last handful of every band, 84 to 104ms and again at 244 to 264, 324 to
344 and 480 to 500. Those are the releases falling after the next
element was keyed but before any of it was audible, and each puts an
unasked-for element after it.

That heard instant is where the output callback really placed this
element's edge. Where an edge is never placed at all -- the stream
replaced under it and its queue dropped -- the keyer falls back on the
keyed start plus the delay the last edge it did place was given, and
over this device the two come to the same instant: made to use that
estimate throughout, the sweep loses only the release at 344ms. So this
set no longer says much about taking the placed edge over the estimate.
It said more when the estimate was reckoned from a lag saved between
sessions rather than from what the audio had just done.

A burst's first element is the exception, and is measured from its
keyed start. That element the operator began themselves, by closing the
paddle; it is the only one whose beginning is known to them without
hearing it, so a squeeze let go of before it can be heard has still
been let go of during it. Measuring it like the rest loses all six
releases inside the lag, from 4 to 24ms.

None of this is visible from the keyed grid. previous_elt_ts is the
instant the element was keyed -- send_burst used to read the clock at
the top of its loop, a whole inter-element gap earlier for every
element but a burst's first, which the lag happened to paper over.

The set is here because 6c715bb broke exactly this and no sweep noticed.
That commit required a remembered paddle to have been closed during the
element it is remembered from, and a released squeeze is the opposite --
closed from before the element began and let go part way through it --
so none of them got its element until efd5c5c. Restoring the broken
guard today leaves the swap, tap and dit-tap sweeps byte identical, and
takes this one from 125 to 25. So does every other way of breaking it
that has been tried: nothing but this sweep notices any of them.

Summary: summary_squeeze.json


The dah tap scenario
--------------------

    python3 paddle_sweep.py <module> <outdir> tap

The dit paddle held throughout and the dah paddle tapped for 5ms, from
84ms to 164ms. No plots are kept for it here; it is the regression test
for the memory guard. A tap is a paddle closed and released inside one
element, which is what the memory exists for, and that sweep comes out
untouched by the guard -- all 21 runs, every element and every KZD and
KZU identical to what they were before it.


Running them
------------

    python3 paddle_sweep.py <a copy of k4companion named *.py> <outdir> \
        <tap|dit-tap|swap|squeeze>

The harness drives the real Keyer over a virtual clock: paddle events
go in through send_paddle_event on the thread a paddle's device would
report on, send_iambic runs on the keyer's thread a hop later, and the
sidetone edges plotted are the frames next_key_edge actually placed.
Nothing about the keyer is reimplemented.

It takes a copy of the program with a .py suffix because it imports it
as a module.
